# Surveys and Sampling: How Data Is Collected Fairly

> Asking everyone is usually impossible, so statisticians ask a slice
> and trust mathematics to make the slice stand in for the whole. How
> the slice is chosen decides what the survey can honestly say.

## 1. Population, Sample, and Census

[definition]
The **population** is every individual the study is about; a **sample**
is the subset actually studied. A **census** studies the whole
population, while a **sample survey** studies a sample and generalises
from it with quantified uncertainty.
[/definition]

| Term | Meaning | School of 2,000 students |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Population | all units of interest | all 2,000 students |
| Sample | the studied subset | 100 chosen students |
| Sampling frame | the list you draw from | the official roll |
| Census | a 100 percent count | the whole-school election |

A number computed from the sample (the **statistic**, such as the
sample mean) estimates the corresponding **parameter** for the whole
population. The gap between them is **sampling error**, and it shrinks
as the sample grows — but only random selection shrinks bias, at any
sample size.

## 2. Why Not Ask Everyone?

Censuses are slow, costly, and error-prone: India's decennial census
enumerates more than a billion people and takes years of preparation.
A well-chosen sample of a few thousand can estimate national opinion
within a couple of percentage points. Sampling is a deliberate trade —
a small, measurable uncertainty in exchange for speed and cost — and
the whole craft lies in making that uncertainty honest.

## 3. The Sampling Family Tree

@diagram tree id="sampling-methods-tree" layout="tree" aria="Probability and non-probability sampling methods"
  "Sampling methods" -> "Probability sampling (known chance of selection)"
  "Sampling methods" -> "Non-probability sampling (chance unknown)"
  "Probability sampling (known chance of selection)" -> "Simple random"
  "Probability sampling (known chance of selection)" -> "Systematic"
  "Probability sampling (known chance of selection)" -> "Stratified"
  "Probability sampling (known chance of selection)" -> "Cluster"
  "Non-probability sampling (chance unknown)" -> "Convenience"
  "Non-probability sampling (chance unknown)" -> "Judgement"
  "Non-probability sampling (chance unknown)" -> "Quota"
  "Non-probability sampling (chance unknown)" -> "Snowball"
@/diagram

Only the left branch lets probability theory measure reliability. The
right branch is cheaper and sometimes the only option, but its bias
cannot be measured away, whatever the sample size.

## 4. Simple Random Sampling

Every sample of size n has the same chance of being chosen — a lottery
with every name in the drum exactly once.

[example]
A class list of 20 students numbered 1 to 20 must yield 4 winners.
Read two-digit pairs from a random number table: 07, 42 (skip — over
20), 13, 91 (skip), 05. The winners are students 7, 13, and 5, plus
the next valid pair drawn. Every student had an equal chance and the
dice did the choosing, not a person.
[/example]

- Pros: free of favouritism; the theory of probability guarantees
  unbiasedness on average.
- Cons: needs a complete frame, and small samples can miss subgroups
  by pure luck — 4 draws can easily land on all boys.

## 5. Systematic Sampling

List the frame, compute the interval, draw one random start, then take
every k-th unit:

@math label="eq:sampling-interval"
k = \frac{N}{n}
@/math

[example]
Choose 25 students from a list of 200.
Step 1: k = 200/25 = 8.
Step 2: pick a random start between 1 and 8 — say 3.
Step 3: the sample is students 3, 11, 19, 27, ... , 195 — 25 names
spread evenly through the whole list.
[/example]

[tip]
Systematic sampling is the field surveyor's favourite: one random
number, then mechanical counting along a street, ward, or queue. Its
only trap is a list that hides a repeating cycle matching k — for
example, every 8th house on a street being a corner shop.
[/tip]

## 6. Stratified Sampling

Split the population into non-overlapping **strata** that differ in
the characteristic under study — class, gender, region — then draw a
random sample from each in proportion to its size.

[example]
A school has 800 grade-9, 700 grade-10, and 500 grade-11 students.
For a sample of 100, each stratum contributes n × (stratum size / N):
Step 1: grade 9 gets 100 × 800/2000 = 40.
Step 2: grade 10 gets 100 × 700/2000 = 35.
Step 3: grade 11 gets 100 × 500/2000 = 25.
Every grade is now represented in exactly its fair share.
[/example]

- Pros: guarantees every subgroup appears in proportion, and gives
  more precise estimates than simple random sampling of the same size.
- Cons: needs the full frame plus reliable sizes for every stratum.

@chart bar title="A proportional stratified sample of 100 students" labels:
Grade 9
Grade 10
Grade 11

data:
40
35
25
@/chart

## 7. Cluster Sampling

When the population is scattered, list natural groups — villages,
wards, schools — choose a few whole groups at random, and study
everyone inside them (one-stage) or sample within each chosen group
(two-stage).

[example]
A district has 50 wards with about 500 households each. Building a
frame of 25,000 households is hopeless, so pick 5 wards at random and
survey 100 households in each: 5 × 100 = 500 interviews, and the only
frame needed is the list of 50 wards.
[/example]

| Method | How units are drawn | Best when | Main risk |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Simple random | lottery over all units | small, complete frames | subgroups missed by luck |
| Systematic | every k-th after a random start | long lists, field work | hidden cycles matching k |
| Stratified | random within each stratum | known, contrasting subgroups | needs full frame and stratum sizes |
| Cluster | random whole groups | scattered population, no frame | similar clusters inflate error |

## 8. Non-Probability Samples and Bias

- **Convenience**: whoever is easiest — mall visitors, your own
  friends, people who answer the door.
- **Judgement**: the researcher hand-picks "typical" cases.
- **Quota**: fixed counts per category, filled non-randomly.
- **Snowball**: respondents recruit the next respondents — invaluable
  for rare or private populations, hopeless for representativeness.

| Bias | What goes wrong |
| --- | --- |
| Selection / undercoverage | the frame omits part of the population (phone polls miss the phone-less) |
| Non-response | the unwilling differ systematically from the willing |
| Response / wording | leading questions push answers ("Should the canteen finally serve healthy food?") |
| Voluntary sample | only the motivated reply, inflating extreme views |

[warning]
The 1936 Literary Digest poll collected 2.4 million ballots — an
enormous sample — and still predicted the wrong US president, because
its list reached wealthier voters far more often. A biased large
sample loses to an unbiased small one every single time.
[/warning]

[important]
Sample size fixes precision; sample selection fixes truth. No sample
size can rescue a method that never gave part of the population any
chance to be heard.
[/important]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Whole group under study | population |
| List used to draw the sample | sampling frame |
| Interval k in systematic sampling | k = N/n |
| 100 from strata of 800 / 700 / 500 | 40 / 35 / 25 |
| Method for scattered villages | cluster sampling |
| A sample mean estimates | the population parameter |
| 2.4 million ballots, wrong prediction | selection bias (1936) |
| Leading question causes | response (wording) bias |

[summary]
Fair sampling is random at some stage: simple random, systematic,
stratified, and cluster each solve a different practical problem, and
the pros-and-cons table tells you which; non-probability shortcuts
save money and buy bias instead; and honest question wording plus a
high response rate protect the result end to end.
[/summary]
